--TEST--
`Core\Program::implementing` takes an interface or a class and refuses every other type argument, an enum and a shape among them, and refuses a listed class whose constructor the enumeration could not call, under an interface and under a class alike, each at the call that would have written the `new` (`rule:programs/implementing`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/core-program-implementing-i-every-class-that-is-an-i
// `rule:programs/implementing`: the type argument is what gives the enumerated
// instances a static type, and the enumeration writes `new C()` for each of
// them. So a type argument that is neither an interface nor a class, and a
// class the enumeration could not construct, are both refused where the call
// is written. The accepted calls are first, so an enumeration that stopped
// answering a well-formed one fails here rather than as a missing refusal.
interface Check {
    public function name(): string;
}

class Fine implements Check {
    public function name(): string { return "fine"; }
}

class Plain {
    public function name(): string { return "plain"; }
}

enum Level { Low = 1, High = 2 }

interface Needful {
    public function label(): string;
}

// What a class needs arrives through the interface's own methods, because the
// enumeration has nothing to pass to a constructor.
class Needy implements Needful {
    public string $label;
    public function constructor(string $label) { $this->label = $label; }
    public function label(): string { return $this->label; }
}

class Sender {}

class NeedySender extends Sender {
    public int $port;
    public function constructor(int $port) { $this->port = $port; }
}

echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<Check>()), "\n";

// A concrete class is accepted, and is in its own list.
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<Plain>()), "\n";

// An enum is neither an interface nor a class.
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<Level>()), "\n";

// A shape describes data rather than methods, so nothing could be called on
// the array it would answer.
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<{name: string}>()), "\n";

// Every class the enumeration instantiates needs a constructor callable with
// no arguments, whether it is listed through an interface or through a class.
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<Needful>()), "\n";
echo Core\Arr::count(Core\Program::implementing<Sender>()), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0743]: `Core\Program::implementing` enumerates an interface or a class, and `Level` is neither
   --> case.nvs:48:22
%A
error[E0743]: `Core\Program::implementing` enumerates an interface or a class, and `{name: string}` is neither
   --> case.nvs:52:22
%A
error[E0744]: `Needy` is a `Needful` and its constructor takes 1 required argument(s)
   --> case.nvs:56:22
%A
error[E0744]: `NeedySender` is a `Sender` and its constructor takes 1 required argument(s)
   --> case.nvs:57:22
%A
